I've capped images you attach yourself at 20 MB. The size is checked before the file is opened, so an oversized image is never loaded. All four checks passed: build, test, clippy with -D warnings, and fmt. Committed as e5458788.

Measured on the built binary: a 300 MB --image now uses 26 MB of memory, prints error: big.png is too large to attach as an image (300.0 MB, max 20 MB); it was not read, and exits 1. The assessment measured 4.7 GB before.

• Where the cap lives: in a new module, src/image_cap.rs. read_image_for_add now reads through its read_image_capped. I re-ran the grep and found no other callers, so this one reader covers all four doors: --image (two call sites in main.rs), /add and @path.
• Same rule as the model's own tool: yoagent 0.25.10's read_file uses a 20 MiB limit with a strict >. yoyo now matches it: a file of exactly 20 MiB is allowed, and one byte more is refused. yoagent's constant is private, so I copied the number and added a comment pointing to src/tools/file.rs:9/:196.
• Behaviour that didn't change: a missing file still gets its old failed to read error, and FIFOs are still refused as before. On an error, --image already printed it and exited 1, so I left main.rs alone. The path in the new message is cleaned of control characters before display.
• Why a new module: commands_file.rs is at its size limit, so the change there is net 0 lines and the logic and tests went into the new file.
• Tests: a boundary table (cap−1, cap, cap+1), the exact message text, control-character cleaning, and a full refusal on a sparse file one byte over 20 MiB (no real 20 MB file is written). Two cases must still pass: a small .png reads exactly as before, and a missing file keeps its error.
• Positive control: with the cap switched off, the 4 refusal tests failed and the 2 must-still-pass tests stayed green. I then restored the cap.
• Docs: one line in CHANGELOG (Unreleased → Fixed), one sentence in docs/src/usage/repl.md, and a Day 234 note in the commands_file.rs entry of ARCHITECTURE.md.

